Now I have a thorough understanding of the ecosystem. Let me design the complete implementation plan.

---

# Clippy for macOS -- Electron + TypeScript Implementation Plan

## 1. Build Tooling Decision: Electron Forge + Vite

**Choice: `electron-forge` with the Vite + TypeScript template.**

Rationale:
- Electron Forge is the officially recommended tool by the Electron team. It handles packaging, code signing, and DMG creation out of the box.
- Vite provides fast HMR during development and clean ESM bundling, which matters because `active-win` is a pure ESM package that needs a bundler to work cleanly in Electron's main process.
- The Forge Vite template gives you a working `forge.config.ts` with no additional wiring.

Scaffold command:
```bash
npx create-electron-app@latest clippy-mac --template=vite-typescript
```

This produces a working app immediately. From there, strip out the default renderer HTML and CSS and replace with the popup UI.

## 2. Project Structure

```
clippy-mac/
  forge.config.ts              # Electron Forge config (makers, plugins)
  tsconfig.json                # Root TS config
  package.json

  src/
    main/
      main.ts                  # Electron entry point: app lifecycle, tray, IPC
      tray.ts                  # Tray icon creation, context menu
      popup-window.ts          # Floating BrowserWindow management
      observer.ts              # Active window polling loop
      claude.ts                # Claude API client + prompt engineering
      settings.ts              # Settings read/write + safeStorage for API key
      context-buffer.ts        # Accumulates window observations between check-ins
      types.ts                 # Shared types for IPC messages

    preload/
      preload.ts               # contextBridge exposing IPC to renderer

    renderer/
      index.html               # Popup HTML shell
      popup.ts                 # Popup UI logic (vanilla TS + DOM)
      popup.css                # Popup styling
      settings.html            # Settings page HTML
      settings.ts              # Settings page logic

    assets/
      tray-icon.png            # 20x20 menubar icon
      tray-icon@2x.png         # 40x40 retina menubar icon
      clippy.png               # Clippy avatar for popup
```

**Why this structure**: Clean separation between main process (Node.js land: API calls, window observation, tray) and renderer (browser land: popup display). The `preload/` directory is the bridge. No framework in the renderer -- vanilla TypeScript and DOM manipulation is sufficient for two small pages (popup + settings).

## 3. Active Window Observation: osascript, not active-win

**Choice: Direct `osascript` calls via Node's `child_process.execFile`.**

Rationale against `active-win`:
- `active-win` is a pure ESM package with native bindings. It introduces complexity: native rebuild steps during packaging, potential Electron version mismatches, and ESM/CJS friction.
- For a macOS-only novelty app, a 3-line AppleScript call is simpler, zero-dependency, and trivially debuggable.

The AppleScript to run:

```applescript
tell application "System Events"
  set frontApp to name of first application process whose frontmost is true
  try
    tell process frontApp
      set windowTitle to name of front window
    end tell
  on error
    set windowTitle to ""
  end try
end tell
return frontApp & "|" & windowTitle
```

Implementation in `observer.ts`:

```typescript
import { execFile } from 'node:child_process';
import { promisify } from 'node:util';

const exec = promisify(execFile);

const APPLESCRIPT = `
tell application "System Events"
  set frontApp to name of first application process whose frontmost is true
  try
    tell process frontApp
      set windowTitle to name of front window
    end tell
  on error
    set windowTitle to ""
  end try
end tell
return frontApp & "|" & windowTitle
`;

export interface WindowInfo {
  appName: string;
  windowTitle: string;
  timestamp: number;
}

export async function getActiveWindow(): Promise<WindowInfo> {
  const { stdout } = await exec('osascript', ['-e', APPLESCRIPT]);
  const [appName, windowTitle] = stdout.trim().split('|', 2);
  return {
    appName: appName ?? 'Unknown',
    windowTitle: windowTitle ?? '',
    timestamp: Date.now(),
  };
}
```

**Permission note**: On macOS 10.15+, the app will need Accessibility permission. The first `osascript` call to System Events triggers the system prompt. This is unavoidable and fine for a menubar utility. The `Info.plist` should include `NSAppleEventsUsageDescription`.

## 4. Renderer Approach: Vanilla TypeScript + DOM

**Choice: No React. Vanilla TypeScript with direct DOM manipulation.**

Rationale:
- The popup is a single small card: an avatar image, a text bubble, and a dismiss button. This is 20-30 lines of DOM code.
- The settings page is a form with 4-5 fields. No state management complexity.
- Eliminating React removes ~40KB from the bundle and one entire layer of build complexity.
- This keeps the app feeling lightweight, which is an explicit constraint.

The popup HTML structure:

```html
<!-- index.html -->
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="./popup.css">
</head>
<body>
  <div id="popup">
    <img id="clippy-avatar" src="../assets/clippy.png" alt="Clippy" />
    <div id="speech-bubble">
      <p id="message-text"></p>
    </div>
    <button id="dismiss">OK</button>
  </div>
  <script type="module" src="./popup.ts"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

The renderer TypeScript receives messages via the preload bridge:

```typescript
// popup.ts
const messageEl = document.getElementById('message-text')!;
const dismissBtn = document.getElementById('dismiss')!;

window.clippy.onMessage((text: string) => {
  messageEl.textContent = text;
});

dismissBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
  window.clippy.dismiss();
});
```

## 5. IPC Architecture

Three-layer pattern: main process <-> preload (contextBridge) <-> renderer.

**preload.ts** -- the only code that touches both worlds:

```typescript
import { contextBridge, ipcRenderer } from 'electron';

contextBridge.exposeInMainWorld('clippy', {
  // Renderer -> Main
  dismiss: () => ipcRenderer.send('popup:dismiss'),
  openSettings: () => ipcRenderer.send('settings:open'),
  saveSettings: (settings: unknown) => ipcRenderer.send('settings:save', settings),
  getSettings: () => ipcRenderer.invoke('settings:get'),

  // Main -> Renderer
  onMessage: (callback: (text: string) => void) => {
    ipcRenderer.on('clippy:message', (_event, text) => callback(text));
  },
});
```

**IPC channels** (defined in `types.ts` for type safety):

| Channel | Direction | Payload | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| `clippy:message` | main -> renderer | `string` | New Clippy commentary to display |
| `popup:dismiss` | renderer -> main | none | User clicked dismiss |
| `settings:open` | renderer -> main | none | Open settings window |
| `settings:save` | renderer -> main | `Settings` | Persist new settings |
| `settings:get` | renderer -> main | returns `Settings` | Load current settings |

**Types file:**

```typescript
// types.ts
export interface Settings {
  apiKey: string;
  checkInIntervalMs: number;   // default: 300_000 (5 min)
  personality: string;          // "classic" | "snarky" | "helpful" | "custom"
  customSystemPrompt?: string;
  enabled: boolean;
}

export interface WindowInfo {
  appName: string;
  windowTitle: string;
  timestamp: number;
}

export interface ContextEntry {
  appName: string;
  windowTitle: string;
  startTime: number;
  endTime: number;
  durationMs: number;
}
```

## 6. Check-In Loop: Context Accumulation and Smart Triggering

This is the core logic. It lives in `context-buffer.ts` and is orchestrated from `main.ts`.

### Observation Layer (every 10 seconds)

A fast polling interval (10s) captures what you are doing. This does NOT call Claude on every poll. It just logs transitions.

```typescript
// context-buffer.ts
export class ContextBuffer {
  private entries: ContextEntry[] = [];
  private current: { appName: string; windowTitle: string; startTime: number } | null = null;

  record(info: WindowInfo): void {
    // If same app+window, extend the current entry
    if (this.current &&
        this.current.appName === info.appName &&
        this.current.windowTitle === info.windowTitle) {
      return; // still on the same thing
    }

    // Finalize previous entry
    if (this.current) {
      this.entries.push({
        appName: this.current.appName,
        windowTitle: this.current.windowTitle,
        startTime: this.current.startTime,
        endTime: info.timestamp,
        durationMs: info.timestamp - this.current.startTime,
      });
    }

    // Start new entry
    this.current = {
      appName: info.appName,
      windowTitle: info.windowTitle,
      startTime: info.timestamp,
    };
  }

  flush(): ContextEntry[] {
    // Finalize current entry if exists
    if (this.current) {
      const now = Date.now();
      this.entries.push({
        appName: this.current.appName,
        windowTitle: this.current.windowTitle,
        startTime: this.current.startTime,
        endTime: now,
        durationMs: now - this.current.startTime,
      });
      this.current = {
        appName: this.current.appName,
        windowTitle: this.current.windowTitle,
        startTime: now,
      };
    }

    const flushed = this.entries;
    this.entries = [];
    return flushed;
  }
}
```

### Check-In Layer (configurable, default 5 minutes)

Every N minutes, the main loop:
1. Flushes the context buffer
2. Formats the entries into a readable summary
3. Sends to Claude API
4. Shows the popup with Claude's response

**Suppression logic** -- do NOT show a popup when:
- The user is in a full-screen video call (app name contains "Zoom", "Meet", "FaceTime" -- configurable blocklist)
- The user has been idle (same window for the entire interval, suggesting AFK)
- The popup is already showing and hasn't been dismissed
- The app was JUST installed and has no context yet (first interval)

```typescript
// In main.ts check-in loop
function shouldSuppress(entries: ContextEntry[]): boolean {
  if (entries.length === 0) return true;

  // Blocked apps
  const blocked = ['zoom.us', 'facetime', 'google meet'];
  const lastEntry = entries[entries.length - 1];
  if (blocked.some(b => lastEntry.appName.toLowerCase().includes(b))) return true;

  // Likely AFK: single entry spanning the whole interval
  if (entries.length === 1) {
    const total = entries[0].durationMs;
    if (total > settings.checkInIntervalMs * 0.9) return true;
  }

  return false;
}
```

## 7. Claude API Integration and Prompt Design

**File: `claude.ts`**

```typescript
import Anthropic from '@anthropic-ai/sdk';

let client: Anthropic | null = null;

export function initClaude(apiKey: string): void {
  client = new Anthropic({ apiKey });
}

const PERSONALITIES: Record<string, string> = {
  classic: `You are Clippy, the classic Microsoft Office assistant, reimagined for macOS in 2026.
You observe what applications and windows the user has open and make brief, charming commentary.
You are enthusiastic, slightly annoying in an endearing way, and always trying to be helpful.
Your responses are 1-2 sentences max. You use the catchphrase "It looks like you're..." frequently.
You occasionally make puns. You are self-aware that you are a novelty and lean into it.`,

  snarky: `You are a snarky desktop assistant who observes what the user is doing on their Mac.
You make brief, witty, slightly judgmental observations about their app usage patterns.
Keep it to 1-2 sentences. Be funny, not mean. Think dry humor, not cruelty.`,

  helpful: `You are a gentle productivity assistant. You observe the user's app usage and offer
brief, genuinely useful observations or tips. 1-2 sentences max. Be warm and supportive.`,
};

export async function getCheckIn(
  entries: ContextEntry[],
  personality: string,
  customPrompt?: string,
): Promise<string> {
  if (!client) throw new Error('Claude client not initialized');

  const systemPrompt = customPrompt ?? PERSONALITIES[personality] ?? PERSONALITIES.classic;

  // Format context into a readable summary
  const contextSummary = entries
    .map(e => {
      const mins = Math.round(e.durationMs / 60_000);
      const duration = mins > 0 ? `${mins}m` : `${Math.round(e.durationMs / 1000)}s`;
      return `- ${e.appName}: "${e.windowTitle}" (${duration})`;
    })
    .join('\n');

  const message = await client.messages.create({
    model: 'claude-sonnet-4-20250514',
    max_tokens: 150,
    system: systemPrompt,
    messages: [
      {
        role: 'user',
        content: `Here's what I've been doing on my Mac in the last few minutes:\n\n${contextSummary}\n\nGive me a brief Clippy-style check-in comment.`,
      },
    ],
  });

  const textBlock = message.content.find(b => b.type === 'text');
  return textBlock?.text ?? "It looks like you're using a computer!";
}
```

**Why Claude Sonnet, not Opus**: This is a lightweight novelty check-in. Sonnet is faster, cheaper, and perfectly capable of generating a one-liner. The `max_tokens: 150` hard cap prevents runaway responses. Each check-in costs roughly $0.001-0.003.

**No streaming**: The response is 1-2 sentences. Streaming would add complexity for zero perceptible benefit. The entire API call takes ~1-2 seconds.

## 8. Floating Popup Window

**File: `popup-window.ts`**

```typescript
import { BrowserWindow, screen } from 'electron';
import path from 'node:path';

let popupWindow: BrowserWindow | null = null;

export function createPopupWindow(): BrowserWindow {
  if (popupWindow && !popupWindow.isDestroyed()) {
    return popupWindow;
  }

  const { width: screenWidth } = screen.getPrimaryDisplay().workAreaSize;

  popupWindow = new BrowserWindow({
    width: 320,
    height: 160,
    x: screenWidth - 340,      // Bottom-right corner, 20px margin
    y: 60,                      // Below menubar
    frame: false,                // No title bar
    transparent: true,           // Allow rounded corners via CSS
    resizable: false,
    movable: true,               // Let user drag it
    alwaysOnTop: true,
    skipTaskbar: true,           // Don't show in dock
    show: false,                 // Show only when we have content
    hasShadow: true,
    vibrancy: 'popover',         // macOS translucent background
    webPreferences: {
      preload: path.join(__dirname, '../preload/preload.js'),
      contextIsolation: true,
      nodeIntegration: false,
    },
  });

  popupWindow.loadFile(path.join(__dirname, '../renderer/index.html'));

  // Hide dock icon -- this is a menubar-only app
  // (set in main.ts via app.dock.hide())

  popupWindow.on('closed', () => {
    popupWindow = null;
  });

  return popupWindow;
}

export function showPopup(message: string): void {
  const win = createPopupWindow();
  win.webContents.send('clippy:message', message);
  win.showInactive(); // Show without stealing focus
}

export function hidePopup(): void {
  if (popupWindow && !popupWindow.isDestroyed()) {
    popupWindow.hide();
  }
}
```

Key design decisions for the popup:
- **`showInactive()`** instead of `show()` -- critical for a non-intrusive experience. The popup appears without stealing keyboard focus from whatever app the user is in.
- **`frame: false` + `transparent: true`** -- allows the CSS to control the visual shape (rounded corners, speech bubble styling).
- **`vibrancy: 'popover'`** -- gives the native macOS frosted glass effect behind the popup content.
- **`skipTaskbar: true`** -- no Dock icon for the popup window.
- **Position: top-right** -- below the menubar, away from common work areas. Similar to macOS notification placement.

## 9. Tray Icon and Context Menu

**File: `tray.ts`**

```typescript
import { Tray, Menu, nativeImage } from 'electron';
import path from 'node:path';

let tray: Tray | null = null;

export function createTray(callbacks: {
  onToggle: () => void;
  onCheckNow: () => void;
  onSettings: () => void;
  onQuit: () => void;
}): Tray {
  const icon = nativeImage.createFromPath(
    path.join(__dirname, '../assets/tray-icon.png')
  );

  tray = new Tray(icon);
  tray.setToolTip('Clippy');

  const contextMenu = Menu.buildFromTemplate([
    { label: 'Check in now', click: callbacks.onCheckNow },
    { type: 'separator' },
    {
      label: 'Enabled',
      type: 'checkbox',
      checked: true,
      click: callbacks.onToggle,
    },
    { label: 'Settings...', click: callbacks.onSettings },
    { type: 'separator' },
    { label: 'Quit Clippy', click: callbacks.onQuit },
  ]);

  tray.setContextMenu(contextMenu);
  return tray;
}
```

The tray icon should be a simple 20x20 PNG (with a 40x40 `@2x` variant for Retina). A paperclip emoji rendered as a template image works well. Template images on macOS automatically adapt to light/dark menubar.

## 10. Settings and API Key Storage

**File: `settings.ts`**

```typescript
import { app, safeStorage } from 'electron';
import fs from 'node:fs';
import path from 'node:path';

const SETTINGS_PATH = path.join(app.getPath('userData'), 'settings.json');

interface PersistedSettings {
  checkInIntervalMs: number;
  personality: string;
  customSystemPrompt?: string;
  enabled: boolean;
  encryptedApiKey?: string;  // Base64-encoded encrypted key
}

const DEFAULTS: PersistedSettings = {
  checkInIntervalMs: 300_000,
  personality: 'classic',
  enabled: true,
};

export function loadSettings(): PersistedSettings {
  try {
    const raw = fs.readFileSync(SETTINGS_PATH, 'utf-8');
    return { ...DEFAULTS, ...JSON.parse(raw) };
  } catch {
    return { ...DEFAULTS };
  }
}

export function saveSettings(settings: PersistedSettings): void {
  fs.writeFileSync(SETTINGS_PATH, JSON.stringify(settings, null, 2));
}

export function storeApiKey(key: string): void {
  if (!safeStorage.isEncryptionAvailable()) {
    throw new Error('Encryption not available');
  }
  const encrypted = safeStorage.encryptString(key);
  const settings = loadSettings();
  settings.encryptedApiKey = encrypted.toString('base64');
  saveSettings(settings);
}

export function retrieveApiKey(): string | null {
  const settings = loadSettings();
  if (!settings.encryptedApiKey) return null;
  if (!safeStorage.isEncryptionAvailable()) return null;

  const buffer = Buffer.from(settings.encryptedApiKey, 'base64');
  return safeStorage.decryptString(buffer);
}
```

**Why `safeStorage` instead of keytar**: keytar is unmaintained (archived December 2022). Electron's built-in `safeStorage` API uses the macOS Keychain under the hood, requires zero extra dependencies, and is actively maintained by the Electron team.

Settings are stored as plain JSON in `~/Library/Application Support/clippy-mac/settings.json`. Only the API key is encrypted. Everything else (interval, personality) is plain text -- there is no security concern with those values.

## 11. Main Process Orchestration

**File: `main.ts`** -- this is the entry point that wires everything together:

```typescript
import { app, ipcMain } from 'electron';
import { createTray } from './tray';
import { createPopupWindow, showPopup, hidePopup } from './popup-window';
import { getActiveWindow, type WindowInfo } from './observer';
import { ContextBuffer } from './context-buffer';
import { initClaude, getCheckIn } from './claude';
import { loadSettings, saveSettings, storeApiKey, retrieveApiKey } from './settings';

// Menubar-only app: hide dock icon
app.dock?.hide();

let observeInterval: ReturnType<typeof setInterval> | null = null;
let checkInInterval: ReturnType<typeof setInterval> | null = null;
const contextBuffer = new ContextBuffer();

app.whenReady().then(() => {
  const settings = loadSettings();

  // Initialize Claude if API key exists
  const apiKey = retrieveApiKey();
  if (apiKey) {
    initClaude(apiKey);
  }

  // Create tray
  createTray({
    onToggle: () => toggleEnabled(),
    onCheckNow: () => performCheckIn(),
    onSettings: () => openSettingsWindow(),
    onQuit: () => app.quit(),
  });

  // Preload the popup window (hidden)
  createPopupWindow();

  // Start observation loop
  if (settings.enabled && apiKey) {
    startLoops(settings.checkInIntervalMs);
  }

  // IPC handlers
  ipcMain.on('popup:dismiss', () => hidePopup());
  ipcMain.on('settings:open', () => openSettingsWindow());
  ipcMain.on('settings:save', (_event, newSettings) => {
    if (newSettings.apiKey) {
      storeApiKey(newSettings.apiKey);
      initClaude(newSettings.apiKey);
      delete newSettings.apiKey;
    }
    saveSettings(newSettings);
    restartLoops(newSettings.checkInIntervalMs);
  });
  ipcMain.handle('settings:get', () => {
    const s = loadSettings();
    return { ...s, encryptedApiKey: undefined, hasApiKey: !!retrieveApiKey() };
  });
});

function startLoops(intervalMs: number): void {
  // Poll active window every 10 seconds
  observeInterval = setInterval(async () => {
    try {
      const info = await getActiveWindow();
      contextBuffer.record(info);
    } catch {
      // osascript failed -- user may have denied permission, skip
    }
  }, 10_000);

  // Check in with Claude at the configured interval
  checkInInterval = setInterval(() => performCheckIn(), intervalMs);
}

function stopLoops(): void {
  if (observeInterval) clearInterval(observeInterval);
  if (checkInInterval) clearInterval(checkInInterval);
  observeInterval = null;
  checkInInterval = null;
}

function restartLoops(intervalMs: number): void {
  stopLoops();
  startLoops(intervalMs);
}

async function performCheckIn(): Promise<void> {
  const entries = contextBuffer.flush();
  const settings = loadSettings();

  if (shouldSuppress(entries)) return;

  try {
    const message = await getCheckIn(entries, settings.personality, settings.customSystemPrompt);
    showPopup(message);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Check-in failed:', err);
  }
}

function shouldSuppress(entries: ContextEntry[]): boolean {
  if (entries.length === 0) return true;
  const blocked = ['zoom.us', 'facetime', 'webex', 'microsoft teams'];
  const last = entries[entries.length - 1];
  if (blocked.some(b => last.appName.toLowerCase().includes(b))) return true;
  // Likely AFK
  if (entries.length === 1 && entries[0].durationMs > loadSettings().checkInIntervalMs * 0.9) return true;
  return false;
}

function toggleEnabled(): void { /* toggle settings.enabled, start/stop loops */ }
function openSettingsWindow(): void { /* create second BrowserWindow for settings.html */ }
```

## 12. Popup CSS -- Making It Delightful

The popup should feel like a native macOS notification crossed with Clippy's speech bubble:

```css
/* popup.css */
* {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  box-sizing: border-box;
}

body {
  background: transparent;
  font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'SF Pro Text', sans-serif;
  -webkit-app-region: drag;   /* Allow dragging the popup */
  user-select: none;
}

#popup {
  display: flex;
  align-items: flex-start;
  gap: 12px;
  padding: 16px;
  background: rgba(30, 30, 30, 0.85);
  backdrop-filter: blur(20px);
  border-radius: 14px;
  border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
  color: #f0f0f0;
  font-size: 13px;
  line-height: 1.4;
  max-width: 320px;
  box-shadow: 0 8px 32px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.4);
}

#clippy-avatar {
  width: 40px;
  height: 40px;
  flex-shrink: 0;
  -webkit-app-region: no-drag;
}

#speech-bubble {
  flex: 1;
}

#message-text {
  margin-bottom: 8px;
}

#dismiss {
  -webkit-app-region: no-drag;
  position: absolute;
  top: 8px;
  right: 8px;
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
  border: none;
  color: #aaa;
  font-size: 11px;
  padding: 2px 8px;
  border-radius: 4px;
  cursor: pointer;
}

#dismiss:hover {
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.2);
  color: #fff;
}
```

## 13. Build and Packaging for macOS

In `forge.config.ts`:

```typescript
import type { ForgeConfig } from '@electron-forge/shared-types';
import { MakerDMG } from '@electron-forge/maker-dmg';
import { VitePlugin } from '@electron-forge/plugin-vite';

const config: ForgeConfig = {
  packagerConfig: {
    name: 'Clippy',
    icon: './src/assets/app-icon',  // .icns file for macOS
    appBundleId: 'com.yourname.clippy',
    osxSign: {},                     // Code signing (optional for personal use)
    darwinDarkModeSupport: true,
    extendInfo: {
      LSUIElement: true,             // Hide from Dock (menubar-only app)
      NSAppleEventsUsageDescription:
        'Clippy needs accessibility access to see which app you are using.',
    },
  },
  makers: [
    new MakerDMG({
      format: 'ULFO',
    }),
  ],
  plugins: [
    new VitePlugin({
      build: [
        { entry: 'src/main/main.ts', config: 'vite.main.config.ts' },
        { entry: 'src/preload/preload.ts', config: 'vite.preload.config.ts' },
      ],
      renderer: [
        { name: 'popup', config: 'vite.renderer.config.ts' },
      ],
    }),
  ],
};

export default config;
```

Key packaging details:
- **`LSUIElement: true`** in `Info.plist` -- this is what makes it a menubar-only app with no Dock icon. Combined with `app.dock?.hide()` in code.
- **`NSAppleEventsUsageDescription`** -- required for the System Events AppleScript call.
- **MakerDMG** produces a standard `.dmg` for distribution.

Build commands:
```bash
pnpm run make          # Build + package + create DMG
pnpm run package       # Build + package only (no DMG)
```

## 14. Dependencies (package.json)

```json
{
  "name": "clippy-mac",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "main": ".vite/build/main.js",
  "scripts": {
    "start": "electron-forge start",
    "package": "electron-forge package",
    "make": "electron-forge make",
    "lint": "tsc --noEmit"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "@anthropic-ai/sdk": "^0.39.0"
